    "## 1. Two ways of thinking: statistics vs. machine learning\n",
    "\n",
    "There is a real cultural difference between the two communities:\n",
    "\n",
    "- The **statistical** way of thinking says: *first formulate a problem, then go\n",
    "  get the data to solve it.* Here **data is cheap and the question is expensive** -\n",
    "  you are paid for asking the right question.\n",
    "  \n",
    "- The **machine-learning** way of thinking says: *here is the data, tell me what it\n",
    "  is telling you.* Here **the question is cheap and the data is expensive** - you are\n",
    "  paid for collecting and exploiting the data.\n",
    "\n",
    "The classic interview deadlock captures it:\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Business:** \"Here is my data. What can you say?\"\n",
    "> **Statistician:** \"What do you want to know?\"\n",
    "> **Business:** \"That's why I want to hire *you*.\"\n",
    "> **Statistician:** \"If you don't tell me what you want to know, how do I know what to tell you?\"\n",
    "\n",
    "Neither side is wrong. Real work lives in between. Take a concrete example: a watch\n",
    "company's sales are falling. You can't answer *\"why?\"* by staring at numbers - you\n",
    "have to **frame questions first**: Which segments are declining? How fast? Where is\n",
    "the bulk of sales? Only then does the data become useful."
   ]
