    "## 5. Standard Deviation\n",
    "\n",
    "### The idea in one line\n",
    "Standard deviation measures **how spread out** numbers are around their average - the *typical distance from the middle*. Low = consistent and predictable. High = wildly spread out.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why the average isn't enough\n",
    "Two things can share the **same average** but feel totally different.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Pizza prices at two restaurants (both average `~$15`):**\n",
    "- **Restaurant A** - every pizza is around `$15`. *Low* standard deviation: consistent, no surprises.\n",
    "- **Restaurant B** - a $2 slice and a `$100` caviar pizza. *High* standard deviation: wild swings.\n",
    "\n",
    "The average alone can't tell them apart. The standard deviation can.\n",
    "\n",
    "### How to calculate it (5 steps)\n",
    "Example - five ages: **20, 22, 24, 26, 28**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Mean:** (20+22+24+26+28) ÷ 5 = **24**\n",
    "2. **Distance from mean:** −4, −2, 0, +2, +4\n",
    "3. **Square each** (so minuses don't cancel pluses): 16, 4, 0, 4, 16\n",
    "4. **Average them** -> this is the **variance**: 40 ÷ 5 = **8**\n",
    "5. **Square root** (back to normal units): √8 ≈ **2.83**\n",
    "\n",
    "So people sit about **2.8 years** from the average age, give or take. (Square root with the root = standard deviation; without it = variance.)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Real-life example: two coffee shops\n",
    "Both shops have an average wait time of 5 minutes. So by the average alone, they look the same. But:\n",
    "\n",
    "Shop A is consistent - you almost always wait about 5 minutes.\n",
    "Shop B is chaotic - sometimes 1 minute, sometimes 12.\n",
    "\n",
    "Standard deviation is the number that exposes this difference. Let me run it so you can see real output."
   ]
  },
