    "## 7. Percentile , Quartiles, IQR (Interquartile Range)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Percentile** = what % of people fall below a point. *\"75th percentile age = 32\"* means 75% of customers are younger than 32.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Quartiles** = the 25%, 50%, 75% cut points (Q1, median, Q3) that split data into 4 equal quarters.\n",
    "\n",
    "**IQR (Interquartile Range)** = Q3 − Q1 = the age span of the **middle 50%** of people. For us, 32 − 24 = **8 years**.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why we use them\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. They ignore outliers (this is the big one).**\n",
    "The mean and standard deviation get yanked around by extreme values. Drop one 100-year-old into the data and the *average* age jumps - but Q1, median, and Q3 barely budge, because they only care about *position in the lineup*, not the actual extreme number. So percentiles describe the real \"typical\" picture honestly.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. They answer \"where do I stand?\"**\n",
    "A raw number (\"age 32\") means little on its own. \"32 = the 75th percentile\" instantly tells you that person is older than three-quarters of customers. That ranking is what exam scores, salary bands, and growth charts all use.\n",
    "\n",
    "**3. IQR is a clean measure of spread that survives messy data.**\n",
    "Standard deviation answers \"how spread out?\" but squares distances, so outliers inflate it. IQR answers the same question using only the middle 50%, so a few weird values can't distort it.\n",
    "\n",
    "**4. They power automatic outlier detection.**\n",
    "The fence rule — anything beyond Q1 − 1.5×IQR or Q3 + 1.5×IQR — is one of the most common, robust ways to flag unusual values (like our 45–50 year-old customers) before they wreck an analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "### When to use them (vs. mean + standard deviation)\n",
    "\n",
    "| Situation | Best tool | Why |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Data is **skewed** or has **outliers** (incomes, house prices, ages with a long tail) | **Percentile / IQR** | Resistant to extremes — gives the honest center and spread |\n",
    "| Data is roughly **symmetric / bell-shaped**, no big outliers | **Mean + std dev** | Cleaner, and unlocks the 68–95–99.7 rule |\n",
    "| You need a **ranking** (\"top 10%\", \"above average height for age\") | **Percentile** | Directly tells you position |\n",
    "| You want to **flag anomalies** automatically | **IQR fences** | Standard, robust outlier rule |\n",
    "| You're drawing a **box plot** | **Quartiles + IQR** | The box *is* Q1–Q3, the whiskers are the fences |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Rule of thumb\n",
    "\n",
    "> If the data is **clean and symmetric → mean + standard deviation.**\n",
    "> If the data is **skewed or has outliers → median + IQR / percentiles.**\n",
    "\n",
    "In our age data, the few 45–50 year-olds pull the mean up, so the **median (27) and IQR (24–32)** describe the typical customer more truthfully than mean (28.5) ± std (7) does. That's exactly why both sets of numbers exist — you pick the one that won't lie about your particular data."
   ]
